<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samerina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from c. 720 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samerina was the Assyrian province created from the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> in 722–720 BCE, with Samaria as its seat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> deported part of the population and settled people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and elsewhere in their place. The province later passed under Babylonian and then Persian rule, when it was known as Samaria.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Samerina was formed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-v">Shalmaneser V</a>'s conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, the northern kingdom, which ended with the capture of its capital, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>. The siege has been tentatively dated to 725 or 724 BCE and its end to 722 BCE, near the close of Shalmaneser's reign. It was the signature event of that reign, the only one the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicles</a> record for it, and it is also told in 2 Kings. The province is first documented under his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>, who was credited with naming it, the first known use of the capital's name for the whole region.</p><p>In several inscriptions Sargon claims to have conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> himself. One explanation is that he refers to a second capture, during the revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> that followed his failure in 720 BCE to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> from Marduk-apla-iddina, led by Yahu-Bihdi, the governor of Hamath, with support from Arpad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Sumur and Samaria. It has also been suggested that Sargon finished a siege still under way when Shalmaneser died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sargon claimed to have deported 27,280 people, mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Guzana</a> in the Assyrian heartland and to the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> in the east. Since the Medes were conquered only in 716 BCE, six years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> fell, the relocation seems to have been planned for years. Such <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> were standard Assyrian policy towards defeated peoples, who were generally moved with their families and belongings and treated as valued members of the empire, while people from other parts of the empire were settled in their place.</p><p>Archaeological surveys show that in Iron Age III many settlements in the region, above all in its north, ceased to be inhabited, while in southern Samaria Assyrian military posts were set up and existing settlements were largely resettled by deportees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, Cuthath, Hamath and Sepharvaim. Assyrian rule went largely unchallenged for the next century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Medo-Babylonian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 609 BCE, which ended the Assyrian monarchy, Samerina passed under Babylonian control. In Jewish history the resettlement is known as the Assyrian captivity, and it forms the basis of the narrative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-lost-tribes">Ten Lost Tribes</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samerina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sargon II claimed the conquest, crushed a further revolt in 720 BCE, deported some 27,000 inhabitants to Mesopotamia and Media, settled people from other lands in their place, and made Samaria the centre of the Assyrian province of <b>Samerina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
